WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.519
And now great moments in unintended

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:03.520
consequencePart one: driving

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.529
days. The year

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.839
1989, the problem,

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.729
terrible air quality in Mexico

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:13.649
City, the solution

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:15.239
prohibit one out of five cars from

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.009
driving each weekday based on their

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:18.749
license plate numbers to keep

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:20.579
traffic down and improve air

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:21.899
quality.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.759
Sounds like a great idea with the

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.019
best of intentions.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.269
What could possibly go wrong?

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:28.889
Citizens got around the ban by

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:30.869
buying a second car often

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:33.329
cheaper, older and less efficient.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:34.979
Having more crappier cars in

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:36.509
circulation failed to fix the

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.069
problem, and according to a number

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:39.839
of studies, air pollution actually

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.579
increased after the restrictions

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:42.539
were imposed.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:43.559
But not to worry.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.089
Politicians saw the error of their

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:47.789
ways and in 2008 expanded

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:49.709
the program to Saturdays.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:51.269
Yes, really.

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:53.729
Part two: Boat taxes.

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:55.859
The year 1773,

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.259
the problem Britain needs money.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.179
The solution collects port and

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:01.949
lighthouse for use on merchant ships

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:04.409
based on their length and width.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:06.209
Sounds like a great idea with the

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:07.349
best of intentions.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:09.329
What could possibly go wrong?

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:10.919
Shockingly, for the British, people

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.869
don't like paying taxes since

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:14.219
ships were charged by width and

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:15.599
length, but not depth.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.999
Shipbuilders maximize cargo capacity

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.189
while minimizing taxes by building

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:22.379
deep, sluggish, flat bottom,

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:24.119
flat sided vessels.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:27.059
Our recipe for instability.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.099
So while Britain's Navy was ruling

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.929
the seas there, unsightly

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:32.579
and unmanageable merchant ships were

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:33.749
a laughing stock.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:35.369
But on the plus side, the taxman

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:36.719
can't reach you on the bottom of the

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.129
ocean.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:39.989
Part three Flying the empty

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:41.969
skies the year

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:43.199
2020.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.059
The problem a global pandemic

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.589
straining the American airline

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:47.579
industry.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:49.799
The solution a $60

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.839
billion bailout to rescue airlines

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.629
and maintain continuity of service.

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.429
Sounds like a great idea with the

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:57.719
best of intentions.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:00.149
What could possibly go wrong?

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:01.799
With concerns over COVID, air

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.689
passenger rates plummeted as much as

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:05.549
95 percent, but

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:07.139
said airlines were mandated to

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:08.879
maintain a minimum level of service

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:10.409
to qualify for the emergency

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:11.339
government funding.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:12.929
Airlines were forced to continue

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:14.849
flying planes even if there was

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:16.389
no one on board.

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:17.249
Voila!

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:18.959
American skies were quickly filled

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:20.819
with ghost flights, nearly

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:22.409
empty planes crisscrossing the

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:23.729
country so the industry could

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:25.949
qualify for billions of taxpayer

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:27.059
funds.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.219
Jet fuel wasn't the only thing being

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:30.220
wasted.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.099
Great moments and unintended

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.419
consequences.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:34.619
Good intentions.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:35.790
Bad results.

